<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="ai_buildout_backlash::financing_cost_datactr" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Shortfalls or volatility in fuel/electricity supplies raise projected operating risk and backup‑power needs for energy‑intensive data centres, prompting lenders and financiers to charge higher all‑in borrowing costs.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_buildout_backlash</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="ai_buildout_backlash::investor_sentiment_ai" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Repeated or anticipated energy disruptions increase expected OPEX and risk for cloud/AI providers, lowering expected returns and causing investors to reduce appetite for AI‑linked assets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_buildout_backlash</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="global_market_repricing::headline_inflation_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">An improvement in energy supply stability quickly lowers wholesale and retail energy prices and caps passthrough to consumer energy bills, directly reducing headline CPI readings.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_market_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="global_market_repricing::nominal_govt_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Greater energy supply stability reduces the need for fiscal backstops and emergency borrowing and narrows sovereign risk premia, putting downward pressure on benchmark government bond yields.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_market_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stability" target="us_iran_escalation::chokepoint_vulnerability" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained disruption of Russian energy exports drives European and global buyers to increase LNG and oil imports from the Gulf, raising traffic and strategic exposure through the Strait of Hormuz and thereby increasing chokepoint vulnerability to U.S.–Iran tensions.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_escalation</data>
</edge>
